<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">code</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Generating Your Project</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            Once your model is ready, Singularity turns it into a complete, working PHP web service &mdash;
            managed-object subclasses, model files, and the project scaffolding, all from your design.
        </p>
    </header>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Save your model</h2>
    <p>
        Choose <em>Save</em> from the main menu to write your model to disk. Saving updates the project's
        model file so your changes are persisted with the project folder.
    </p>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Generate code</h2>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>Create ManagedObject Subclass&hellip;</strong> &mdash; generates a PHP subclass for an entity, giving you typed access to its attributes and relationships.</li>
        <li><strong>Show SQL Schema&hellip;</strong> &mdash; opens the SQL schema derived from your model, so you can review the tables and columns it will produce.</li>
        <li><strong>Import&hellip;</strong> &mdash; brings an existing model into the project.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">lightbulb</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Never write boilerplate by hand.</strong>
            The generated classes and files follow the Sabatier conventions automatically. Design the model,
            regenerate, and the code stays in step with your data.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Where to go from here</h2>
    <p>
        That's the core loop: design entities, add attributes and relationships, then generate. To revisit
        the basics, return to
        <a class="help-link" href="#welcome" data-help-anchor="welcome">Welcome to Singularity</a>.
    </p>
</article>
